People who've never endured major depression simply
won't believe you when you tell them your will can
be just as paralyzed as a spinal injury victim's
body.

Not bathing is no more a voluntary choice than is
any other aspect of the results of my mental
illness; the part of my brain that is supposed to
"volunteer" me for such luxuries as cleanliness
isn't working.

If I focus all of my attention for maybe three solid
days on finding some clean clothes, carrying them to
the bathroom, and bathing, I can sometimes manage;
usually that means I get the clean clothes _found_
one day, moved to the bathroom to sit in the dry
sink the next day, and bathe the third day.

It doesn't help that I'm easily distracted by other
equally hard calls to head like hunger or a full
bladder or bowel, calls that are even more insistant.

You get used to the soles of your feet coming off in
your socks after while (though I will _never_ get
used to walking on feet with no calluses, feet the
soles of which are often cracked and bleeding; that
involves real pain), and the rest of your body being
coated with a heavy slime of self-produced oils, but
as to how I stand it? I have no functional desire to
feel good; I deeply despise myself and find this
justice inflicted on me by the universe.  If
breathing weren't an involuntary impulse, I couldn't
be bothered to do that, either.

Depression means you set your own comfort and well
being at value zero, _expect_ and _accept_ and
sometimes self-destructively _arrange_ that your
life will be absolutely miserable.

    [It takes real effort to get yourself divorced
    by someone whose life you saved from cancer by
    marrying her and using good, military retiree
    insurance to get her to competent very rare
    cancer specialists, but I managed the trick
    handily. She's long outlived her expected
    lifespan of two years, our thirteenth
    anniversary would have been in three weeks, her
    incurable cancer is kept in remission more
    easily with medicines developed during our
    marriage, but in the end she outlived her
    ability to put up with me too.]

I hear the shower calling to me constantly, I can
only struggle mutely to answer. I _hate_ being
dirty, always have; that's not the issue.

Yes, it sucks to be me, but I gave up ideas of
suicide the first time I was locked away.

